  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Foreign policy is the sum of external decisions taken in pursuit of the national interest. Domestic law, a ministry's budget and protocol are instruments, not the policy itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The national interest persists while governments and ambassadors change. Party politics affects style, not the core interest.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Security, territorial integrity, economic development and prestige are the four components. The others are not Nigeria's interests to define.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Afrocentrism places Africa at the centre. Nigeria belongs to the UN, the Commonwealth and OPEC, which rules out the other options.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The three principles are the standard formula. Nigeria was non-aligned during the Cold War and joined the major organisations.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The national interest is the centre; West Africa, Africa and the world are successive circles. The question asks for the circle closest to the centre, and it is the interest itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    ECOWAS, whose treaty was revised in 1993. The OAU was established by its own charter in 1963, the AU in 2002, and the Commonwealth is older than all three.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1975. The OAU was founded in 1963, ECOMOG intervened in Liberia in 1990, and the AU replaced the OAU in 2002.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Addis Ababa, which remained its headquarters and is now the seat of the African Union.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2002, at Durban. The Constitutive Act was adopted in 2000, but the Union itself began work in 2002.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1960, on independence. The UN itself was founded in 1945, before Nigeria existed as an independent state.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1971. OPEC itself was founded in 1960, and 1975 is ECOWAS.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    OPEC uses oil production and pricing as leverage, which makes it economic. It is not a cultural, military or judicial body.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ECOWAS Cease-fire Monitoring Group, deployed to Liberia in 1990 and later to Sierra Leone, with Nigeria as its backbone.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1990, at the height of the Liberian civil war. 1975 is ECOWAS's founding and 1999 the return to civilian rule in Nigeria.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nigeria sent troops to the UN mission in the Congo in 1960, immediately after independence. Liberia and Sierra Leone came later, under ECOMOG.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1987. It sends Nigerian professionals to African, Caribbean and Pacific states and is the standard example of the cultural instrument.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An ambassador represents the sending state and its head of state. The other offices belong to other institutions.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Ministry of Foreign Affairs, headed by the Minister of Foreign Affairs, coordinates the missions and advises the President.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The embassy, the ambassador and the note are the diplomatic machinery. The army is military, the tariff economic, and the festival cultural.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sanctions restrict trade or finance in order to change behaviour, which makes them economic. Diplomacy and culture work through persuasion and exchange.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The executions under Abacha triggered the suspension, which lasted until 1999 and the return to civilian rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is voluntary and mostly, but not exclusively, made up of former British territories. Mozambique and Rwanda joined without that history.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nigeria avoided formal membership of either superpower bloc while trading with both. It did not leave the UN or stop trading.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oil dependence ties the foreign-policy budget to the world price. Population and membership of the Commonwealth and the AU are assets, not limits.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The economy depends on oil, so a fall in price cuts the resources available for diplomacy. Embassies and ECOWAS membership are instruments, not handicaps.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Decolonisation, anti-apartheid, ECOWAS and peacekeeping are the standard achievements. The other options are not Nigerian policies.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1994–95. Nigeria has served more than once, and 1994–95 is the term the textbooks cite.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sovereign equality is the formal equality of states in international law. The veto and the power of the strong qualify it in practice, which is the tension the paper likes.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nigeria campaigned for sanctions and isolation, which is part of the anti-apartheid record. The other options contradict that policy.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2019, after a period of hesitation about the effect on domestic manufacturing. It is the newest economic instrument in the chapter.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Remittances and advocacy give the diaspora weight, and the state has created institutions to manage the relationship.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Instability and rights criticisms reduce Nigeria's moral standing. Population, ECOWAS and oil are sources of influence, not constraints.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    “Ad hoc” is the criticism: a policy improvised case by case rather than derived from the national interest. Afrocentrism is a doctrine, and multilateral and bilateral describe the form of engagement.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bilateral means two states; multilateral means several, usually through an organisation. A state and an organisation is a mixed case, not the definition.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The General Assembly admits on the Security Council's recommendation, which gives the permanent members a veto over membership.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Geography makes neighbouring instability a direct security and economic threat, which is why West Africa is the second circle.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    ECOMOG's intervention and the diplomacy around it worked together, which is why the case is used to show that instruments are combined.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The executive leads, the foreign ministry coordinates and the legislature appropriates and ratifies. The judiciary interprets and the service implements.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nigeria has been a non-permanent member but does not hold a permanent seat or a veto, which is the reform it has campaigned for.
  ]

]

